    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-39108-z",
    "abstract": "The substantial risks posed to Venice and its lagoon by ongoing and projected sea-level rise (SLR) require unprecedented long-term adaptation strategies. We map the evolution of development pathways and the progressive shrinking of the solution space as SLR advances, identifying adaptation tipping points and analysing the relative pros and cons of alternative measures. The analysis highlights trade-offs among environmental quality, heritage preservation, social well-being and relevant Sustainable Development Goals, and costs increasing with SLR. With present insufficient greenhouse gas mitigation policies, the current open lagoon strategy, with mobile barriers and multiple accommodation measures, is likely to encounter hard limits within the current century. Follow-up strategies include ring-dikes isolating the city from the rest of the lagoon, or a closed lagoon with permanent coastal dams, each preserving different combinations of values while entailing major ecological and socio-cultural transitions. Under extreme SLR, relocation of monuments to suitable inland areas and abandonment would be the only remaining strategy, which might become unavoidable in the 22nd century under current climate policies and an Antarctic ice-sheet collapse. Rapid mitigation could still avoid the most disruptive long-term outcomes.",
    "field": "Coastal adaptation to sea-level rise",
    "problem": "The current adaptation strategy for Venice, featuring mobile barriers, is becoming insufficient and unsustainable due to accelerating sea-level rise, creating complex trade-offs between environmental quality, heritage preservation, and social well-being that require evaluating more radical, long-term pathways like permanent closure or retreat.",
    "method": "This paper maps long-term adaptation pathways for Venice against sea-level rise, identifying tipping points and analyzing trade-offs among strategies like mobile barriers, ring-dikes, a closed lagoon, and retreat to show how the solution space progressively shrinks over time.",
    "task": "The task is to map the evolution of long-term adaptation pathways for Venice under sea-level rise by identifying adaptation tipping points and analyzing the trade-offs, pros, cons, and costs of alternative strategies like mobile barriers, ring-dikes, a closed lagoon, and retreat.",
